A||||5||Bautsch||Low temp thermostat???
Z||000000||Bautsch||08-21-99||12:33 PM||||I need to replace my thermostat, when I went to purchase one I had a choice between the OEM and a low temp version. Why would I choose to put a low temp thermostat in versus the OEM?<p>Thanks,<br>Mike
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||08-21-99||02:14 PM||||I'm not sure but I think it has something to do with the way the ECM controls the mixture. Up until a certain temperature, the mixture will be rich (more fuel, less air). As soon as the engine hits 150F (I think), the ECM switches to normal mixture. When the mixture is rich, you'll notice a slight performance increase (better acceleration because of the fuel increase) but it will be tougher on the engine (cold metal is less flexible than hot metal).<p>The thermostat will be closed as long as the optimal operating temperature is not reached. This way, the cooling fluid will heat up relatively fast. As soon as that temperarature is reached it will open and cooling fluid is rerouted through the radiator, cooling it down. If no thermostat is installed, the cooling fluid will always go through the radiator and will heat up very slowly, sometimes never reaching the optimal temperature. The result of this is that the ECM will never switch to normal mixture. You'll have a better performance all the time, but you are killing your engine in the process. A low temp thermostat is sort of an in-between solution. It will keep the temperature (or at least it tries to) just below the point where the ECM switches. But cooling fluid will be a whole lot warmer than when no thermostat is installed resulting in less engine damage.<p>A simpler reason for using a low temp thermostat can be if you live in a place where outside temperatures are high and the chance of overheating the engine is high. The low temp thermostat will simply keep the engine's temp lower.<br>
Z||000002||theogre||08-21-99||03:09 PM||||read thermostat help........ <p>In very short, the ECM is perfectly happy with a 180F stat. It will not tolerate 160 without a new chip.
Z||000003||terryk||08-21-99||11:15 PM||||Hi guys.<p>I want to be diplomatic here, but richer is not usually better. Many people confuse acceleration enrichment with power enrichment. <p>AE is the same as a pump shot. Increasing it (and it is higher at low coolant temps), gives a initial burst that feels good. <p>However, AE times out pretty quick and power enrichment takes over. PE increased the amount of fuel in the mixture. Certainly, at wide open throttle you need more.<p>But when you do things to increase the mixture (fuel pressure, bigger injectors, etc) it gives you that burst when AE starts, but actually slows the car down when PE comes on. <p>Seat-o-the-pants improvements are a deceiving. <p>I reduced the PE in my 86 V6 to bring the O2 down to .8V on the O2 (.95V before) and that really improved the pull. <p>I've been fooled by seat of the pants "improvements". <p>
Z||000004||Bruce||08-22-99||09:39 AM||||Huh??
Z||000005||lowCG||08-22-99||11:41 PM||||What Terry is saying,I believe,is that a change can feel like an improvement because it is only an abrupt transition from what was there before the change was made.<p>The added blast of fuel can give better response at certain throttle openings,however,when opened further,the mixture will be rich,and the cars was actually better to begin with.<p>This can be deceptive when tuning(modified)engines,until you've reached a point where you've done it enough times to tell the difference.<p>It would only be so easy if we had four big knobs to adjust;idle mixture,acceleration quantity,accelleration duration,and wide open.<p>
